f 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166959 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1045 "Fourier Cosine Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166992 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bffb8473-8281-5879-a88a-b0d8ca02dcb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167287 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "464460d1-2c6d-5d78-a455-18bf4796cfeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167287 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{10(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167290 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb68e665-73e2-503c-a3f5-1fe023348ad8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167290 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{3}{2}-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1061 595703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70e9a79d-82de-580b-aef4-7b85b63184e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1061 595704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8aa6cef0-eda2-592b-9aca-4965f3055e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1061 595707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8896eaaa-38ce-50d6-9371-a6e16a56ffbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167937 #edb/ref 1061 595710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1fe41d9-fcaa-5738-8ea0-e820f8f39957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/e2/e2e07048ac4750968947e45443f59096f395b159d4f7a846410a711194b11a14.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1065 14676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9cc2bdd-3ebe-5b87-ad23-c43cf508923b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311967 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311967 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311967 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00fa78b3-f990-59e4-9c5d-d0332b6aff1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312191 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312191 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312191 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1cb713a9-182a-58c5-8920-2a63b83c7f86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312243 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312243 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312243 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "240146db-8d91-59be-b64b-76e1c8746ed1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312273 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312273 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312273 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27402392-ef7b-543e-a95c-51569128931d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312433 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "388c3d71-2ecb-56b2-a8f8-8278a8c14822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313781 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313781 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/re